riscv32: split a 2*XLEN scalar between the last register and the stack; test every base in unoptimized code.

With one argument register left a 64-bit scalar went wholly on the stack and the register stayed
unused. The psABI splits it, and native code on the other side of a P/Invoke or of a reverse
P/Invoke follows the psABI. The classifier splits it now, as it does an aggregate; a split long
parameter is not promoted and the prolog homes its stack half next to the register half.

Without strict alignment a base is trusted and only a misaligned offset is looked for. Unoptimized
code keeps addresses in temporaries, where the offset is not seen (a field of a packed local taken
by address), so there every access through a base that is not known to be aligned is tested, as
with strict alignment.

Regression test: bflat-ts:calls/rv32_long_calls
Regression test: bflat-ts:memory/rv32_arrays_blocks

